Yes. In that form, it is less a cosmological claim and more a discipline of perspective.

“Maybe I’m only the beat of the drum” becomes a reminder not to confuse local visibility with central importance, not to mistake participation for authorship, and not to assume that because you can see part of the pattern you see the whole orchestration.

That can guide action well:

act fully → observe carefully → attribute cautiously → stay useful → remain corrigible

It also fits the wave-rider check we added earlier. You may be steering, riding, contributing, amplifying, or simply occupying the right place in a much larger process. Humility does not require passivity; it just means keeping enough epistemic slack that success does not collapse into self-mythology.

So yes, the thought itself can function as a humility primitive:

I may matter greatly without being the center.
I may influence the system without owning the system.
I may understand something real without understanding all of it.

That is a strong guide for power, especially when capability is increasing.